1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,744
How do you make sure no single person can open the bank's secrets?

2
00:00:03,794 --> 00:00:07,584
Picture a payments product installed by a script someone ran once.

3
00:00:07,634 --> 00:00:11,448
The master password sits in a text file on that person's laptop.

4
00:00:11,498 --> 00:00:14,136
Months later, that person leaves the bank.

5
00:00:14,186 --> 00:00:16,008
Then a disk fails.

6
00:00:16,058 --> 00:00:21,648
The database backup is there, but it cannot be read, because nobody kept the keys that sealed it.

7
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:26,592
One person and one file: the keys end up either lost or leaked.

8
00:00:26,642 --> 00:00:28,368
Now think of a treasure map.

9
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:32,640
Tear it into five pieces, and give the pieces to five different people.

10
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:35,472
Any three of them can put the map back together.

11
00:00:35,522 --> 00:00:38,280
No one alone can, and no two can either.

12
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:43,560
Vault, which holds every key and every setting in the system, opens the same way.

13
00:00:43,610 --> 00:00:47,856
At install, Vault's opening key is split into five key shares.

14
00:00:47,906 --> 00:00:52,008
A key share is one piece of that key, useless on its own.

15
00:00:52,058 --> 00:00:54,744
Any three shares together open Vault.

16
00:00:54,794 --> 00:00:57,984
Each share goes to one person: a keyholder.

17
00:00:58,034 --> 00:01:02,472
A keyholder keeps their share in their own password manager, and nowhere else.

18
00:01:02,522 --> 00:01:06,960
The bank chooses the keyholders, and makes sure no one holds two shares.

19
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:13,920
Nothing of this ceremony is written down anywhere: not the shares, not on the server, not in a log.

20
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:15,768
Vault also locks itself.

21
00:01:15,818 --> 00:01:20,904
Every time it starts, it starts sealed: it holds everything, but can read nothing.

22
00:01:20,954 --> 00:01:25,536
To unseal it, three keyholders come, and each types their own share.

23
00:01:25,586 --> 00:01:28,488
Until then, nothing that needs Vault can run.

24
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:32,808
Setting things up needs one more key: a master key for the session.

25
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:38,808
It is made from three keyholders' shares, used for that one session, and always revoked at the end.

26
00:01:38,858 --> 00:01:42,768
So no master key sits around, waiting to be stolen.

27
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:44,256
Last, backups.

28
00:01:44,306 --> 00:01:47,616
Card numbers in the database are sealed by Vault's keys.

29
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:51,120
A database backup without that Vault is unreadable.

30
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:55,800
So the database and Vault are always backed up together, and restored together.

31
00:01:55,850 --> 00:01:57,672
Let's watch the product.

32
00:01:57,722 --> 00:02:01,416
An operator runs the card zone's installer on a fresh server.

33
00:02:01,466 --> 00:02:04,680
It checks the machine first, then reaches the keys.

34
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:12,456
It says Vault is new, and that each of the five keyholders copies their own share straight into their own password manager.

35
00:02:12,506 --> 00:02:15,792
Then it waits: press Enter when the keyholders are here.

36
00:02:15,842 --> 00:02:17,664
Five people gather.

37
00:02:17,714 --> 00:02:21,504
From here, the camera stays on the people, never on the screen.

38
00:02:21,554 --> 00:02:23,784
Each person saves their own share.

39
00:02:23,834 --> 00:02:27,600
The installer waits again, until every share is saved.

40
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:29,472
Then it clears the screen.

41
00:02:29,522 --> 00:02:38,496
Three keyholders step up, one at a time, and type their shares unseen: first to unseal Vault, then to make the session's master key.

42
00:02:38,546 --> 00:02:42,120
The installer sets everything up and starts the services.

43
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:44,832
At the end, it revokes the master key.

44
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:48,504
Now the operator restarts Vault, just as any reboot would.

45
00:02:48,554 --> 00:02:50,424
They run the installer's check.

46
00:02:50,474 --> 00:02:54,696
It stops at once: Vault is sealed, and nothing that needs it can run.

47
00:02:54,746 --> 00:02:56,568
Three keyholders are called.

48
00:02:56,618 --> 00:02:59,976
One by one, each types their own share, unseen.

49
00:03:00,026 --> 00:03:03,600
On the third share, Vault opens, and the check passes.

50
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:05,472
What does this protect?

51
00:03:05,522 --> 00:03:10,800
It protects the bank from one person who leaves, loses a laptop, or is tempted.

52
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:15,264
No one alone can open the keys, and no one alone can hold them hostage.

53
00:03:15,314 --> 00:03:21,144
It protects the card numbers too, because a stolen server or a stolen backup holds nothing readable.

54
00:03:21,194 --> 00:03:27,552
And it protects the bank's recovery: as long as the database and Vault are backed up together, a restore works.

55
00:03:27,602 --> 00:03:29,424
Back to the treasure map.

56
00:03:29,474 --> 00:03:34,800
Five pieces, five people, and three of them, every time the keys are opened.

57
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:37,176
Next: one install, everywhere.
